### Describe the bug

After upgrading to 3.169.0 from 3.155.1 (specifically PR #13232 which bumped protobufjs from 7.4.0 to 7.5.5), all `int64` fields in OTLP/protobuf ingestion (e.g. intValue, startTimeUnixNano, endTimeUnixNano, timeUnixNano) are serialized as `protobufjs` internal Long objects (`{"low": <int>, "high": <int>, "unsigned": <bool>}`) instead of the previous numeric form. Anything downstream that reads the S3-staged OTLP payload (most notably the server-side ingestion masking callback) receives this shape and breaks if it expects OTLP/JSON-conformant int64 (decimal string per spec, or at least a number).

The endpoint code at `web/src/pages/api/public/otel/v1/traces/index.ts` calls `.toObject(parsed)` without a longs option:

```javascript
  resourceSpans = $root.opentelemetry.proto.collector.trace.v1.ExportTraceServiceRequest
    .toObject(parsed)
    .resourceSpans;
```

In protobufjs 7.4, `util.Long` was not initialized in our build (no long.js resolvable from the protobufjs install location), so `int64` fell back to plain JS number. In protobufjs 7.5, an internal fallback Long class is created when long.js cannot be resolved, but that class does not have a `toJSON()` method — so `JSON.stringify` emits the raw enumerable properties {low, high, unsigned} and they get persisted to S3 / forwarded to the masking callback verbatim.

This is a silent regression: spans still ingest, but downstream consumers that strictly type-check int64 (e.g. pydantic `int`) start failing.

### Steps to reproduce

Reproducible directly inside the official images (no langfuse setup required):
```bash
  docker run --rm --entrypoint sh langfuse/langfuse:3.155.1 -c 'cd /app/web && node -e "
  const pb = require(\"protobufjs\");
  const root = pb.Root.fromJSON({ nested: { T: { fields: { v: { type: \"int64\", id: 1 } } } } });
  const T = root.lookupType(\"T\");
  const dec = T.decode(T.encode({ v: 1234567890 }).finish());
  console.log(JSON.stringify(T.toObject(dec)));
  "'

