### Describe the bug

**SDK: @langfuse/tracing**

When using `startActiveObservation` with an `asType` option (e.g. "chain", "retriever", "embedding"), the `langfuse.observation.type` attribute is never written to the OTel span — so the observation appears as a generic `SPAN` in Langfuse instead of the specified type.

### Root cause

In `LangfuseBaseObservation`, the constructor only calls `createObservationAttributes` (which writes `OBSERVATION_TYPE`) when `params.attributes` is truthy:

```
if (params.attributes) {
  this.otelSpan.setAttributes(
    createObservationAttributes(params.type, params.attributes)
  );
}
```

`startActiveObservation` creates typed observations without passing attributes:

```
case "chain":
  observation = new LangfuseChain({ otelSpan: span });
  break;
case "retriever":
  observation = new LangfuseRetriever({ otelSpan: span });
  break;
```
So `params.attributes` is always `undefined`, the guard fails, and `OBSERVATION_TYPE` is never set. The type only gets written if the user happens to call `.update()` inside the callback — which is not documented as a requirement and fails silently.

`startObservation` is unaffected because it passes attributes explicitly.

### Steps to reproduce

### To reproduce

```
startActiveObservation("my-retriever", async () => {
  // do work — never call obs.update()
}, { asType: "retriever" });
// → appears as SPAN in Langfuse, not RETRIEVER
```

### Workaround

Call `.update({})` as the first line of the callback to force the type attribute to be written:

```
startActiveObservation("my-retriever", async (obs) => {
  obs.update({});
  // do work
}, { asType: "retriever" });
```

### Expected behaviour

`startActiveObservation` should write `langfuse.observation.type` immediately on span creation, regardless of whether the callback calls `.update()`. The fix would be to unconditionally call `createObservationAttributes` in `LangfuseBaseObservation`'s constructor:

```
this.otelSpan.setAttributes(
  createO

## Summary

When tracing the Anthropic Claude Agent SDK via the [OpenInference Claude Agent SDK instrumentation](https://github.com/Arize-ai/openinference/tree/main/js/packages/openinference-instrumentation-claude-agent-sdk) + `@langfuse/otel`, the **authoritative LLM cost is correctly captured as the OTel attribute `llm.cost.total` on the AGENT span**, but Langfuse ignores it and displays a derived cost from `llm.token_count.*` × the pricing table. That derived value is materially under-counted (~40% of actual) because the OpenInference instrumentation only emits the last-turn input tokens + total output tokens — it does not emit `llm.token_count.prompt_cache_read` or `llm.token_count.prompt_cache_creation`, which dominate input usage on multi-turn agent runs that use Anthropic prompt caching.

Expected: when an OTel span carries `llm.cost.total`, Langfuse should use that as the authoritative cost (or, at minimum, surface it alongside the computed value and let users prefer it). Today it's silently dropped.

