## Summary

When a ClickHouse insert exhausts its retry budget, `ClickhouseWriter` **discards the records**. In an observability product this is silent telemetry loss on the primary write path — the data is acknowledged upstream but never lands.

A `TODO` on the line acknowledges the gap. What makes this worth raising as an issue is that the dead-letter infrastructure it asks for **already exists and is wired up** — it just isn't reachable from this path.

Observed on `main` @ `ae84d6a39` (v4.3.1).

## Affected code

`worker/src/services/ClickhouseWriter/index.ts:509-548`

```js
queueItems.forEach((item) => {
  if (item.attempts < this.maxAttempts) {
    entityQueue.push({ ...item, attempts: item.attempts + 1 });
  } else {
    // TODO - Add to a dead letter queue in Redis rather than dropping
    recordIncrement("langfuse.queue.clickhouse_writer.error");
    droppedCount++;
  }
});
```

Records past `maxAttempts` are counted (`langfuse.queue.clickhouse_writer.rows_dropped`) and their ids logged, then dropped. The observability around the loss is good; the loss itself is unrecoverable.

## The DLQ infrastructure already exists

- `QueueName.DeadLetterRetryQueue` — `packages/shared/src/server/queues.ts:411`
- `DlqRetryService.retryDeadLetterQueue` — `worker/src/services/dlq/dlqRetryService.ts`
- Registered and running — `worker/src/app.ts:613-617`:

```js
DeadLetterRetryQueue.getInstance();
WorkerManager.register(
  QueueName.DeadLetterRetryQueue,
  DlqRetryService.retryDeadLetterQueue,
  ...
```

So this looks less like "build a DLQ" and more like "route the exhausted-retry branch into the DLQ that's already there."

## Why this matters

- **Data loss is silent from the client's perspective.** The ingestion API has already returned success by the time the writer gives up, so nothing signals the SDK or the user that spans went missing.
- **Failures are correlated, not random.** The retry budget is most likely to be exhausted during a ClickHouse incident or a sustaine

## Summary

`handleCommentMentionNotification` batches project users into a lookup map to avoid per-user queries, then immediately does a per-user `prisma.notificationPreference.findUnique()` **inside the mention loop**. A comment mentioning N users issues N sequential preference queries.

Observed on `main` @ `ae84d6a39` (v4.3.1).

## Affected code

`worker/src/features/notifications/commentMentionHandler.ts`

The batching intent is explicit at line ~120:

```js
// Create lookup map for O(1) access
const userMap = new Map(projectUsers.map((u) => [u.id, u]));
```

and the loop even references it — "from our single query above" at line ~143. But then, at line 161:

```js
for (const userId of mentionedUserIds) {
  ...
  // Check notification preference (default: enabled)
  const preference = await prisma.notificationPreference.findUnique({
    where: {
      userId_projectId_channel_type: {
        userId, projectId, channel: "EMAIL", type: "COMMENT_MENTION",
      },
    },
  });
```

The preference lookup is the one query that didn't get the batching treatment the rest of the function received.

## Why this matters

- **Inconsistent with the code immediately above it.** The author clearly established the batch-then-map pattern; this is a single missed call rather than a design choice.
- **Latency is serial.** Each iteration also awaits `buildCommentLink` and `sendCommentMentionEmail`, so a comment mentioning a whole team serialises a round trip per person on top of the query.
- **The unique key is a natural `in` filter.** `(userId, projectId, channel, type)` with fixed `projectId`/`channel`/`type` collapses cleanly to a single `findMany`.

## Suggested fix

Hoist the preference lookup out of the loop, mirroring the existing `userMap` pattern:

```js
const preferences = await prisma.notificationPreference.findMany({
  where: {
    projectId,
    channel: "EMAIL",
    type: "COMMENT_MENTION",
    userId: { in: mentionedUserIds },
  },
});
