## Summary

Model-name uniqueness within a project is enforced by an application-level `findFirst` check plus two **sentinel column values** written solely to make a partial DB constraint engage. The pre-check is a read-then-write, so it doesn't hold under concurrency.

There's an existing `TODO(LFE-3229)` noting the models-table cleanup that would remove the workaround. I don't have visibility into that ticket, so this issue is mainly to record the concurrency window and ask where it fits.

Observed on `main` @ `ae84d6a39` (v4.3.1).

## Affected code

`web/src/server/api/routers/models.ts:286-318`

```js
// Check if model name is unique within the project
// Note: The database has a uniqueness constraint on (projectId, modelName, startDate, unit),
// but this constraint is not enforced when startDate or unit are NULL.
// We do an explicit check here to ensure uniqueness on just (projectId, modelName).
// TODO(LFE-3229): After models table cleanup, enforce uniqueness constraint directly on (projectId, modelName)
const existingModelName = await tx.model.findFirst({
  where: { projectId, modelName },
});

if (existingModelName && modelId !== existingModelName.id) {
  throw new TRPCError({ code: "BAD_REQUEST", message: `Model name '${modelName}' already exists in project` });
}

const upsertedModel = await tx.model.upsert({
  ...
  create: {
    ...
    startDate: new Date("2010-01-01"), // Set fix start date for uniqueness constraint to work. TODO: drop after cleanup of models table in LFE-3229
    unit: ModelUsageUnit.Tokens,       // Set fix unit for uniqueness constraint to work. TODO: drop after cleanup of models table in LFE-3229
  },
```

## The two problems

**1. The uniqueness check is racy.** `findFirst` → `upsert` is a read-then-write. The surrounding `$transaction` doesn't prevent it: under Postgres' default `READ COMMITTED`, two concurrent creates for the same `(projectId, modelName)` can both see no existing row and both insert. The DB constraint can't ca

## Summary

`createAnnotationQueueAssignment` performs a no-op-tolerant `upsert`, then writes an `action: "create"` audit log **unconditionally**. Re-assigning a user who is already assigned to the queue emits a spurious "create" record into the audit trail.

Observed on `main` @ `ae84d6a39` (v4.3.1).

## Affected code

`web/src/features/annotation-queues/server/publicAnnotationQueueService.ts:588-606`

```js
// Create the assignment (upsert to handle duplicates gracefully)
const assignment = await prisma.annotationQueueAssignment.upsert({
  where: { projectId_queueId_userId: assignmentWhere },
  create: assignmentWhere,
  update: {},          // <-- no-op on conflict
});

// TODO: only create audit log if upsert actually creates a new record
if (auditScope) {
  await auditLog({
    action: "create",
    resourceType: "annotationQueueAssignment",
    resourceId: assignment.id,
    ...
    after: assignment,
  });
}
```

`update: {}` means an existing assignment is returned unchanged — no state transition occurred — but the audit log records a creation regardless.

## Why this matters

Audit logs are a compliance surface (`AuditLog` is a first-class Prisma model, and there's a `feat-audit-logs` area and a `compliance` label in this repo). Their value depends on entries corresponding to real state changes.

The concrete failure: an operator reviewing "when was this user granted access to this queue?" sees multiple `create` entries with different timestamps and cannot distinguish the actual grant from later idempotent re-assignments. Since this endpoint is reachable from the public API, a client that re-syncs assignments on a schedule would inflate the trail on every run.

It's a small bug, but it makes the audit log say something that isn't true.

