### Describe the bug

`truncate()` in `packages/shared/src/utils/stringChecks.ts` cuts strings by UTF-16 code units via `String.prototype.substring`, with no awareness of surrogate pairs:

```ts
export function truncate(str: string, n = 16) {
  if (str.length > n) {
    return str.substring(0, n) + "...";
  }
  return str;
}
```

Any character outside the Basic Multilingual Plane — emoji (🚀, 🎉), many CJK extension-B ideographs, mathematical alphanumerics (𝟙), etc. — is stored as two UTF-16 code units (a surrogate pair). When the truncation boundary `n` falls between the two halves of a pair, `substring(0, n)` keeps only the high surrogate. The result is a lone, unpaired surrogate that renders as the Unicode replacement character `�`.

This is user-visible wherever `truncate()` is applied to strings that can contain such characters, including:

- `web/src/features/dashboard/components/ModelCostTable.tsx:90` — `truncate(item.providedModelName as string, 30)` (model names are user-controlled and frequently contain emoji)
- `web/src/features/dashboard/components/LatencyTables.tsx:124,127` — `truncate(item.name as string)` (trace/observation names, default n = 16)
- `packages/shared/src/in-app-agent/server/persistence.ts:1562` — `truncate(normalizedText, 600)`

Whether a given value breaks depends on the offset of the astral character relative to `n`, so it fails intermittently and looks like data corruption to the user.

**Expected:** truncation never splits a code point; output is always well-formed text (worst case, one character shorter).
**Actual:** output can end in a lone high surrogate that displays as `�`.

