### Summary

The OpenAI ChatML adapter (`packages/shared/src/utils/chatml/adapters/openai.ts`) renders only the **first** `output_text` part of an assistant message. When a Responses API message carries multiple `output_text` parts, every segment after the first is silently dropped from the trace chat view.

### Where

`normalizeMessage()` inspects only `content[0]`:

```ts
const firstItem = normalized.content[0];
if (
  firstItem &&
  typeof firstItem === "object" &&
  firstItem.type === "output_text" &&
  typeof firstItem.text === "string"
) {
  // Extract just the text for simple output
  normalized.content = firstItem.text;
}
```

### Reproduction

```ts
const output = {
  output: [
    {
      type: "message",
      role: "assistant",
      content: [
        { type: "output_text", text: "The capital is Paris.", annotations: [] },
        { type: "output_text", text: " It is known for the Eiffel Tower.", annotations: [] },
      ],
    },
  ],
};

openAIAdapter.preprocess(output, "output", {});
// → [{ role: "assistant", content: "The capital is Paris." }]
// The second segment is gone.
```

Multiple `output_text` parts are spec-legal Responses API message content — e.g. text segments around web-search citations / browsing results arrive as separate annotated segments, so real traces can hit this.

### Suggested fix

When **every** item of a content array is an `output_text` part, join their texts (sibling adapters already normalize multi-part text this way — e.g. the AI SDK adapter joins its text items with `""`). Content arrays that mix `output_text` with other part types (e.g. `refusal`) stay untouched, so those non-text parts keep reaching rendering instead of being silently dropped.

Happy to submit a PR if this direction sounds right.

