## Summary

An in-app-agent session's MCP permission level is decided by an HTTP header the client itself sends. `getInAppAgentContext` (`web/src/pages/api/public/mcp/index.ts:195-219`) starts every in-app-agent key at `permissions: "read"`, then reads `IN_APP_AGENT_MCP_TOOL_OVERRIDE_HEADER` from the request, parses `{"toolNames": [...]}`, and upgrades the session to `permissions: "tool-allowlist"` with the client's list. The registry honors the allowlist (`web/src/features/mcp/server/registry.ts:179-185`).

The upgrade is meant for a trusted orchestration flow, but the header is client-controlled on the very connection that holds the in-app-agent key, so the "read or approved tools only" gate is advisory in name and advisory in fact. A holder of the key, or a script in the agent page, can name any tools they want. Any tools at all. No approval needed. RBAC remains intact behind the gate, and tool execution still runs under the user's own roles. The approval UX is the boundary being bypassed here. As shipped it is not a boundary at all.

## Proposal

Move the allowlist decision server-side: derive permitted tools from the run's persisted state (the run row the orchestrator created) rather than a per-request header, or at minimum sign the override payload so the page cannot mint one. If the header must stay for worker-driven continuations, restrict it to server-to-server authentication paths where the browser cannot present it.

## Reproducer

```ts
// web/src/__tests__/server/unit/mcp-route-in-app-agent-context.servertest.ts:51
// already demonstrates the client-chosen upgrade:
req.headers[IN_APP_AGENT_MCP_TOOL_OVERRIDE_HEADER] = '{"toolNames":["upsertDataset"]}';
getInAppAgentContext(req, true)
// → { permissions: "tool-allowlist", allowedToolNames: ["upsertDataset"] }
```

Found during an external review of the current tree.

